WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
If predators eat prey, why do predator numbers keep climbing after the prey have

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.420
started to fall? The answer is in how one kill is counted.

3
00:00:13.533 --> 00:00:16.793
Every population keeps a ledger of births and deaths.

4
00:00:18.153 --> 00:00:20.833
Births push its numbers up, and deaths pull them down.

5
00:00:22.193 --> 00:00:25.113
Here are two ledgers, one for prey and one for predators.

6
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:27.657
Now watch the kills.

7
00:00:29.117 --> 00:00:30.697
Each is a death in the prey's ledger.

8
00:00:32.037 --> 00:00:36.177
Each is also food, and on average, predators turn that food into births.

9
00:00:37.477 --> 00:00:39.077
So every kill is entered twice.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.220
That double entry ties the two ledgers together.

11
00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.420
The more predators there are, the faster each prey dies.

12
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.320
The more prey there are, the faster predators breed.

13
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:54.553
First ask when the prey stop changing.

14
00:00:55.913 --> 00:01:00.693
Each prey's birth rate stays fixed, but its risk of death grows with every predator.

15
00:01:02.053 --> 00:01:04.753
At one predator number, P*, the two balance.

16
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:08.207
Now ask when the predators stop changing.

17
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:12.227
Each predator must eat enough prey to replace itself.

18
00:01:13.467 --> 00:01:15.547
That happens at one prey number, N*.

19
00:01:16.827 --> 00:01:18.627
With more prey, predators increase.

20
00:01:20.047 --> 00:01:21.067
With fewer, they decrease.

21
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.770
Put both thresholds on one picture, with prey across and predators up.

22
00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:30.970
The two lines cut it into four zones.

23
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.230
Each zone has its own pair of directions.

24
00:01:35.383 --> 00:01:39.103
Start a dot in the bottom right, with many prey and few predators.

25
00:01:40.483 --> 00:01:45.343
Following the arrows, it circles the crossing point counter-clockwise, and returns

26
00:01:45.343 --> 00:01:46.343
to where it began.

27
00:01:47.317 --> 00:01:50.157
Stop the dot where the prey curve reaches its top.

28
00:01:51.337 --> 00:01:52.437
The prey have just peaked.

29
00:01:53.697 --> 00:01:55.497
Are the predators rising or falling?

30
00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:07.793
Still rising. The prey are falling, but there are still more of them than N*.

31
00:02:08.713 --> 00:02:12.393
So each predator still eats more than it needs to replace itself.

32
00:02:13.417 --> 00:02:16.577
The predators peak only when the prey drop through N*.

33
00:02:17.777 --> 00:02:20.177
So the predator peak comes after the prey peak.

34
00:02:21.397 --> 00:02:23.457
Nothing in the model builds in a delay.

35
00:02:24.677 --> 00:02:26.317
The lag comes from the bookkeeping.

36
00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.690
Start the dot somewhere else, and it traces a different loop.

37
00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:36.750
In this classical model, each start with both populations present and away from the

38
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:38.730
crossing point keeps its own loop.

39
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:41.910
Where it starts sets the size of the cycle.

40
00:02:43.183 --> 00:02:46.783
So every kill, entered in both ledgers, ties them together.

41
00:02:48.163 --> 00:02:53.343
In this simple model, each population's per capita growth rate is set by the other's

42
00:02:53.343 --> 00:02:53.583
numbers.
